// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5.1, 5.2, 5.3 touch-ups
setP("5.1 What TEMPO captures at air toxics monitors", [
"TEMPO HCHO columns reproduced the seasonal cycle and the broad differences among surface air toxics monitors, and a usable observation was available for about seven of every ten 24 h samples nationally and three of every four in Colorado. Day-to-day agreement was weaker and strongly site dependent: significant at most monitors, with a median correlation near 0.5 at 24 h sites, but ranging from 0.9 to zero. This is the central practical result. A satellite column is informative about the day-to-day variation seen by a typical air toxics monitor, and useless at some, and which of the two a given monitor is cannot be assumed from the network average.",
"Within Colorado, the strongest anomaly correlations were at La Salle and Commerce City on the northern Front Range, and the weakest among long records was at Grand Junction. Colorado's monitors sit below the national median, so whatever limits agreement there is not universal. We can only speculate about the reasons for the differences; they may include the magnitude of synoptic and episodic variability relative to retrieval noise, the representativeness of a monitor for a several-kilometre area, and, at Grand Junction, a valley setting where persistent winter inversions could decouple surface concentrations from the column [[hypothesis — consider meteorological analysis]]. The national distribution gives one clue: agreement is weakest at the lowest-concentration sites (Sect. 4.9), which is what a fixed retrieval noise predicts. The records at Colorado Springs, Pueblo, Cañon City and Wheat Ridge (July or October to December 2025) are too short to characterise day-to-day skill."
]);

setP("5.3 Interpreting the column–surface relationship", [
"In Colorado the effective mixing height was about half the TEMPO PBL height on matched days (0.83 against 1.55 km); nationally the two were close (1.09 against 1.04 km). Three factors bear on the difference between column and surface. First, TEMPO HCHO columns have been reported to be biased low by about 30 % relative to FTIR and Pandora observations (Ortega et al., 2026) [[add site-specific Boulder values and Rawat et al. (2026) high-column bias after checking the papers]]; correcting a 30 % low bias would raise the Colorado median H_{eff} from 0.83 km to about 1.2 km, comparable with the median PBL height. Second, HCHO produced or emitted within the boundary layer and concentrated near the surface yields H_{eff} below the PBL depth. Third, the 24 h surface samples include night-time concentrations that the daytime satellite does not see. Known artefacts of DNPH sampling, such as the negative interference from ozone (Arnts and Tejada, 1989), would lower surface concentrations and raise H_{eff}, so they do not explain low values; most national samples are collected with an ozone scrubber or denuder (Sect. 2.3). Calibration differences among HCHO measurement techniques can also be substantial (Jeong et al., 2026).",
"That Colorado's H_{eff} is low relative to the national value is consistent with its monitors sitting at high elevation in semi-arid terrain, where the modelled boundary layer is deep in summer while HCHO remains concentrated near its sources. The larger seasonal amplitude of the column than of the surface concentration — 3.0-fold against 1.7-fold from winter to summer — is consistent with deeper summer mixing and a larger free-tropospheric HCHO column in summer. The 3 h comparison adds a direct constraint: between 06:00–09:00 and 09:00–12:00 the median TEMPO boundary layer height rises from 0.5 to 1.6 km while the median column and the inferred H_{eff} barely change (0.95 km in both windows), as expected because vertical mixing redistributes HCHO without altering the column."
]);

// 5.2: replace the last sentence about the national extension
{
  const s = S[byHead("5.2 Noise and spatial averaging")];
  s.p = [s.p[0].replace(
    "Where agreement stays far below the noise ceiling, as at Grand Junction, the explanation must lie with the monitor's representativeness or with the vertical structure above it rather than with retrieval noise; neither terrain inside the block nor a winter effect accounts for it, and identifying what does would need meteorological analysis beyond this study [[consider for the national extension]].",
    "Where agreement stays far below the noise ceiling, as at Grand Junction, the explanation must lie with the monitor's representativeness or with the vertical structure above it rather than with retrieval noise; neither terrain inside the block nor a winter effect accounts for it, and identifying what does would need meteorological analysis beyond this study. The national results support the same reading from the other direction: the block-size gradient is reproduced across the country, and the sites where agreement is weakest are those with the lowest concentrations, where a fixed noise consumes the largest share of the variance (Sect. 4.9).")];
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5.4 rewritten
rename("5.4 Sampling time, mixing, and the lag that matters",
       "5.4 Sampling time, mixing, and why the lag that matters is site dependent");
setP("5.4 Sampling time, mixing, and why the lag that matters is site dependent", [
"The Colorado 3 h samples show that the comparison depends on when TEMPO looks relative to when the air was sampled, and that the best match is not the sampling window itself. Samples collected between 06:00 and 09:00 MST correlated poorly with the column measured at the same time, well with the column three hours later, and not at all with the column six hours later. The national sub-daily sites show that this is not a general property of morning samples. At the two California sites, samples collected between 05:00 and 08:00 local time correlate with the column measured during those same hours as well as midday samples do with the midday column. The lag that matters is a property of the site, not of the clock.",
"Three lines of evidence argue against the simplest alternative, that early-morning retrievals are too poor to be useful. At the 24 h sites, where the sample spans the whole day, early scans track the surface as well as later scans (Sect. 4.8). Scan-to-scan noise and reported per-cell uncertainties are similar in the two windows. And the California result settles it directly: an early-morning window that carries no day-to-day information at two Colorado sites carries as much as a midday window at two Californian ones, even though the Californian window begins an hour earlier in the day.",
"A boundary layer explanation fits all of these observations. Between 06:00 and 09:00 on the Front Range the surface layer is shallow and stable, so a surface sample reflects overnight accumulation in a thin layer that is decoupled from most of the column above it; the column at that hour is dominated by the residual layer and the free troposphere. By 09:00–12:00 the mixed layer has deepened — the median TEMPO boundary layer height triples — and has entrained the air that was near the surface during sampling, so the column and the sample describe much the same air mass. By 12:00–15:00, with the mixed layer deeper still, photochemical production, entrainment of free-tropospheric air and horizontal transport have replaced or diluted that air, and the correspondence is lost. On this reading, the lag that maximises agreement is the delay between sampling and the time at which the sampled air becomes part of the mixed layer, which depends on how stable the local morning boundary layer is and how quickly it breaks up.",
"The contrasts within our data are consistent with that. Chatfield State Park lies at the foot of the Front Range, where nocturnal drainage flow and a strongly stable morning layer decouple the surface from the air aloft; its sampling-window correlation is essentially zero and the +3 h window is strong. Platteville sits on the open plains of the Denver–Julesburg basin, where morning decoupling is weaker and the surrounding region is more uniform, and there the sampling window and the +3 h window perform about equally (0.55 and 0.46 for the whole record). Bakersfield and Clovis lie on the floor of the San Joaquin Valley, where the summer morning boundary layer grows early and the samples begin an hour earlier still, and there the sampling window works. This ordering is what a morning-stability explanation predicts, but our data cannot test it directly; co-located boundary layer measurements, or a lag analysis stratified by a stability index, would [[hypothesis — could be tested with boundary layer soundings or modelled stability]].",
"The 8 h sites make the same point on a coarser time grid and with 40 sites rather than four. The block beginning at 12:00 local time, which lies wholly within the well-mixed afternoon, agrees better than the block beginning at 04:00, which spends its first hours in the stable morning layer and its remaining hours after mixing. The difference is real but modest (day-to-day correlations of 0.42 and 0.34), as expected when a window is long enough to average across the transition.",
"The practical implication is therefore not “match the satellite to the sampling window”, nor “add three hours”, but “match it to the hours when the sampled air is part of the mixed layer”, and to determine when those hours are at each site rather than assuming. For 24 h samples the all-day mean is a reasonable compromise, as our results show; for programmes designing sub-daily carbonyl sampling, a window centred on the middle of the day should correspond most closely to what a geostationary instrument sees, at every site. Whatever the choice, the metadata matter: without the sample start times recorded in AQS we would have had the Colorado sampling window backwards, and would have reported the best-fitting window as the sampling window."
]);

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5.6 new, 5.6 -> 5.7
rename("5.6 Implications for air toxics monitoring and limitations",
       "5.7 Implications for air toxics monitoring and limitations");
S.splice(byHead("5.7 Implications for air toxics monitoring and limitations"), 0, {
  h2: "5.6 Colorado in national context, and what a national comparison adds",
  p: [
"Taking the national network as the reference changes how the Colorado results should be read. The pooled correlation across Colorado's seven sites, 0.56, is higher than the national 0.42, but the two are not measuring the same thing. A correlation pooled over sites is a mixture of agreement within sites and agreement about the differences between them, and the mixture depends on how alike the sites are. Colorado's pooled value is close to the median correlation at its individual sites (0.53), because its seven monitors share one regional seasonal cycle; the national pooled value falls well below the median at individual sites (0.42 against 0.64), because monitors in different climates have different column-to-surface ratios. The day-to-day correlation, which removes both the seasonal cycle and the between-site differences, is the quantity that means the same thing in both, and there Colorado sits below the national norm (0.30 pooled and 0.35 as a median over its AQS sites, against 0.40 and 0.48 nationally). A study of a single state would not have known which of these it was seeing.",
"The national comparison also tests the two explanatory claims the case study makes. The first, that averaging blocks help because they average down noise, predicts a block-size gradient everywhere and weaker agreement where concentrations are low; both hold across the country. The second, that the timing of the match is governed by boundary layer development rather than by the quality of morning retrievals, predicts that morning sampling should work at sites where the morning layer grows early; that is what the California sites show. Neither test was available from Colorado alone.",
"Finally, the comparison places these results beside the only previous study of surface and satellite HCHO over the United States. Wang et al. (2022) compared seasonal means of surface HCHO at 45 AQS sites with OMI columns for 2006–2015 and found regional correlations of 0.56–0.87 in summer, weak or negative correlations in winter, and a seasonal amplitude 20–100 % larger in the column than at the surface. Our seasonal findings reproduce theirs — the column amplitude is roughly twice the surface amplitude in Colorado — but the comparison no longer has to be seasonal. With a geostationary instrument, 70 % of individual 24 h samples across the country carry a usable observation, and the same networks can be used to ask when, and at which monitors, a satellite tracks day-to-day variation."
  ]
});

setP("5.7 Implications for air toxics monitoring and limitations", [
"For air toxics programmes, these results suggest that TEMPO can provide spatial and seasonal context between and around 1-in-6 day samples and can flag regional episodes such as smoke, but it cannot substitute for monitor measurements at daily time scales at an individual site — and how close it comes varies enormously between monitors. A programme considering satellite data as a supplement can estimate what to expect at its own sites from the site-level results reported here (Fig. 9) rather than from a network average. Pairing satellite observations with time-resolved surface samples, recording sampling start and end times, and preferring midday sampling windows where the science allows would all make better use of hourly retrievals.",
"Several limitations apply. The record spans two years. In the Colorado case study, four 24 h sites have six months or less of data and the 3 h record at Platteville ends in July 2025; nationally, the sub-daily arm rests on 40 sites with one 8 h sampling pattern and four sites with 3 h samples, two of them the Colorado pair, so the evidence that morning sampling can work rests on two Californian sites in one air basin. Integrated DNPH samples are subject to artefacts and calibration uncertainty. The lags that could be examined were limited by the solar zenith angle screen, which removes the earliest and latest windows entirely, and the 20:00 LST block of the 8 h sites is dark. The national effective mixing heights assume a fixed air temperature of 15 °C (Sect. 3.2). TEMPO V04 HCHO is provisional; scans were assigned to sampling periods by granule midpoint, which can differ from the time TEMPO viewed a given site by tens of minutes; and the PBL height is a model product. Wildfire smoke is classified only in the Colorado case study, and HMS smoke classification is qualitative and column based. Finally, many correlations were computed across sites, seasons and screening variants without correction for multiple comparisons, and anomaly p-values are optimistic; the site-level significance counts in Sect. 4.9 should be read with that in mind."
]);

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6 Conclusions
setP("6 Conclusions", [
"We evaluated TEMPO Level 3 V04 HCHO columns against every integrated formaldehyde sample reported to EPA's Air Quality System in 2024–2025 — 16 383 samples at 123 sites in 37 states — with Colorado's air toxics and ozone-precursor network examined in detail as a case study. Nationally, a screened TEMPO observation was available for 70 % of 24 h samples; the daily column and the 24 h surface concentration correlated at 0.42, and at 0.40 once the shared seasonal cycle and the differences between sites were removed. Agreement was significant at 122 of 141 site–duration combinations but varied from 0.9 to zero between monitors, with a median day-to-day correlation of 0.48 at 24 h sites. It was weakest where concentrations were lowest, and it improved with spatial averaging everywhere; in Colorado, where the noise could be quantified from successive scans, about half the day-to-day variance of a 3 × 3 daily column is retrieval noise, which caps the attainable correlation near 0.7 and accounts for the whole benefit of averaging.",
"The hour at which a sample is collected matters more than its length or the precision of the match. Colorado's 3 h samples, whose 06:00–09:00 MST window is recorded in AQS, agree with TEMPO not during sampling (r = 0.12) but three hours later (0.40), and not at all after six hours (0.03). The national sites show that this lag is a property of the site rather than of the clock: at two California sites the 05:00–08:00 window agrees with the column measured in that same window as well as midday sampling does, and across 40 sites sampling 8 h blocks the midday block outperforms the morning block. The unifying explanation is that a surface sample and the column describe the same air only once the morning boundary layer has entrained the sampled air, at an hour that depends on local morning stability. In the Colorado case study, wildfire smoke raised both surface and column HCHO within season and contributed to day-to-day agreement, most clearly in that same post-sampling window.",
"TEMPO provides useful seasonal and episodic context for air toxics monitoring across the United States, and its value for tracking day-to-day variation is greatest when observations are averaged spatially, when the monitor's concentrations are not close to the retrieval noise floor, and when the satellite is matched to the hours in which the sampled air is mixed through the boundary layer. Which of those conditions hold is a question that can now be answered monitor by monitor."
]);

